\section{IPv6 datagrams, \texttt{sendmsg}, \texttt{MSG\_PEEK} and friends}
\label{sec:sockets:more}

The sockets of the first version were IPv4 only and had the calls that Python and \texttt{getaddrinfo} use. Programs that were not written for this system ask for more.
This section lists what was added later, and why each piece is as small as it is.

\paragraph{\texttt{AF\_INET6}.} The kernel has IPv6 UDP (chapter \ref{ch:network}), so a datagram socket can be an IPv6 socket: \texttt{socket (AF\_INET6, SOCK\_DGRAM, 0)}.
An address of a socket is now the small structure \texttt{struct saddr} (a flag \texttt{v6}, the IPv4 address or the 16 bytes of the IPv6 address, and the port), and the system
call code of chapter \ref{ch:libc} turns a \texttt{sockaddr\_in} or a \texttt{sockaddr\_in6} into it. The network stack keeps IPv4 and IPv6 ports in two tables, so the socket
has a second set of 16 handler functions (\texttt{udp6\_in0} to \texttt{udp6\_in15}, made by the same macro) and a datagram in the queue has room for either kind of sender.
A socket has the family it was made with: an address of the other family is \texttt{EAFNOSUPPORT}, as on Linux; there are no IPv4-mapped addresses and no
\texttt{IPV6\_V6ONLY}. \textbf{TCP over IPv6 is not done}: \texttt{socket (AF\_INET6, SOCK\_STREAM, 0)} is \texttt{EAFNOSUPPORT}, which is what a program expects when it tries
IPv6 first and falls back (musl's \texttt{getaddrinfo} does). The reason is in section 26 of \texttt{ARM64-PORT.md}: the peer address is a \texttt{u32} in about ten places of \texttt{tcp.c}.

\paragraph{\texttt{sendmsg} and \texttt{recvmsg}.} They are \texttt{sendto} and \texttt{recvfrom} with the buffer in pieces (\texttt{iov}) and the address in a structure. The
translation is in chapter \ref{ch:libc}; the sockets do not know about them. Ancillary data is not supported.

\paragraph{\texttt{MSG\_PEEK}.} A datagram stays in the queue (the receive function does not move \texttt{qhead}). A TCP connection cannot take back what it has handed out, so
a peek reads up to 512 bytes into a buffer of the socket (\texttt{pk}) and later calls deliver from there first. \texttt{poll} counts the bytes of the buffer as readable.

\paragraph{\texttt{SO\_ERROR} and \texttt{SO\_RCVTIMEO}.} A non-blocking \texttt{connect} answers \texttt{EINPROGRESS}; later \texttt{poll} says the socket can be written (or
has \texttt{POLLERR}), and the program asks how the connection ended with \texttt{getsockopt (SO\_ERROR)}. \texttt{connect\_check} looks at the connection, and writes a failure
into the socket; reading it once clears it. (The TCP code does not say \emph{why} a connection ended, so a refused connection and a server that does not answer are both \texttt{ECONNREFUSED}.)
\texttt{SO\_RCVTIMEO} is the time (in milliseconds) after which a receive call that waits gives up with \texttt{EAGAIN}; the wait loop already had a time limit, only the value was missing.

\subsection{The test program \texttt{sockext}}
\label{src:libc/sockext.c}

It runs against the servers of \texttt{tools/socket\_test.py} (the TCP and UDP server of \texttt{sockettest}, and a UDP server on \texttt{[::1]} that is reached through qemu's IPv6 router
\texttt{fec0::2}): \texttt{sockext 10.0.2.2 TCPPORT UDPPORT UDP6PORT}.

<<libc/sockext.c>>=
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <poll.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <sys/uio.h>
#include <sys/time.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>

static int fails = 0;
#define CHECK(c, msg) do { if (c) printf ("  user ok:   %s\n", msg); \
                           else { printf ("  user FAIL: %s\n", msg); fails++; } } while (0)

static long now_ms (void) { struct timespec t;  clock_gettime (CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &t);  return t.tv_sec * 1000 + t.tv_nsec / 1000000; }

static int readable (int fd, int ms) { struct pollfd p = { fd, POLLIN, 0 };  return poll (&p, 1, ms) == 1 && (p.revents & POLLIN); }

int main (int argc, char **argv) {
  if (argc != 5) { printf ("usage: sockext HOST TCPPORT UDPPORT UDP6PORT\n");  return 2; }
  struct sockaddr_in tcp = { .sin_family = AF_INET, .sin_port = htons (atoi (argv[2])) };
  struct sockaddr_in closed = { .sin_family = AF_INET, .sin_port = htons (1) };
  struct sockaddr_in6 six = { .sin6_family = AF_INET6, .sin6_port = htons (atoi (argv[4])) };
  inet_pton (AF_INET, argv[1], &tcp.sin_addr);  closed.sin_addr = tcp.sin_addr;
  inet_pton (AF_INET6, "fec0::2", &six.sin6_addr);                    // qemu's IPv6 router, which is the host
  char buf[200];
  int n;

  // IPv6 datagrams: sendto, recvfrom with a sockaddr_in6, connect, getsockname, an IPv4 address is refused
  int u6 = socket (AF_INET6, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
  CHECK (u6 >= 0, "socket (AF_INET6, SOCK_DGRAM) works");
  CHECK (sendto (u6, "ping", 4, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &six, sizeof six) == 4, "IPv6 UDP: sendto a datagram to the host server (fec0::2)");
  CHECK (readable (u6, 5000), "poll: the answer makes the IPv6 socket readable");
  struct sockaddr_in6 from;  socklen_t fl = sizeof from;
  n = recvfrom (u6, buf, sizeof buf, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &from, &fl);
  CHECK (n == 4 && !memcmp (buf, "PING", 4), "IPv6 UDP: recvfrom gives PING");
  CHECK (fl == sizeof from && from.sin6_family == AF_INET6 && from.sin6_port == six.sin6_port && !memcmp (&from.sin6_addr, &six.sin6_addr, 16),
         "recvfrom: the sender is a sockaddr_in6 with the address fec0::2 and the port of the server");
  struct sockaddr_in6 me;  socklen_t ml = sizeof me;
  CHECK (getsockname (u6, (struct sockaddr *) &me, &ml) == 0 && me.sin6_family == AF_INET6 && ntohs (me.sin6_port) >= 1024 && !IN6_IS_ADDR_UNSPECIFIED (&me.sin6_addr),
         "getsockname: AF_INET6, a local port and one of our own addresses");
  errno = 0;
  CHECK (sendto (u6, "x", 1, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &tcp, sizeof tcp) == -1 && errno == EAFNOSUPPORT, "an IPv4 address on an IPv6 socket: EAFNOSUPPORT");
  CHECK (connect (u6, (struct sockaddr *) &six, sizeof six) == 0 && send (u6, "pong", 4, 0) == 4 && readable (u6, 5000) && recv (u6, buf, sizeof buf, 0) == 4 && !memcmp (buf, "PONG", 4),
         "IPv6 UDP: connect, send and recv");

  // sendmsg and recvmsg with two pieces each
  struct iovec out[2] = { { "pi", 2 }, { "ng", 2 } };
  struct msghdr mo;  memset (&mo, 0, sizeof mo);
  mo.msg_name = &six;  mo.msg_namelen = sizeof six;  mo.msg_iov = out;  mo.msg_iovlen = 2;
  CHECK (sendmsg (u6, &mo, 0) == 4, "sendmsg: two pieces \"pi\" and \"ng\" are one datagram of 4 bytes");
  CHECK (readable (u6, 5000), "poll: the answer is there");
  char a[3], b[10];
  struct iovec in[2] = { { a, 3 }, { b, 10 } };
  struct sockaddr_in6 f;
  struct msghdr mi;  memset (&mi, 0, sizeof mi);
  mi.msg_name = &f;  mi.msg_namelen = sizeof f;  mi.msg_iov = in;  mi.msg_iovlen = 2;
  n = recvmsg (u6, &mi, 0);
  CHECK (n == 4 && !memcmp (a, "PIN", 3) && b[0] == 'G', "recvmsg: PING is spread over two pieces (3 and 1 bytes)");
  CHECK (mi.msg_namelen == sizeof f && f.sin6_family == AF_INET6 && f.sin6_port == six.sin6_port, "recvmsg: msg_name has the sender");

  // MSG_PEEK on a datagram: the datagram stays
  CHECK (send (u6, "peek", 4, 0) == 4 && readable (u6, 5000), "a datagram for MSG_PEEK has arrived");
  n = recv (u6, buf, sizeof buf, MSG_PEEK);
  CHECK (n == 4 && !memcmp (buf, "PEEK", 4), "recv (MSG_PEEK) shows the datagram");
  memset (buf, 0, sizeof buf);
  n = recv (u6, buf, sizeof buf, MSG_PEEK);
  CHECK (n == 4 && !memcmp (buf, "PEEK", 4), "...and shows it again: it was not taken");
  n = recv (u6, buf, 2, 0);
  CHECK (n == 2 && !memcmp (buf, "PE", 2), "recv without MSG_PEEK takes it (a buffer of 2 bytes cuts the datagram)");
  errno = 0;
  CHECK (recv (u6, buf, sizeof buf, MSG_DONTWAIT) == -1 && errno == EAGAIN, "now the queue is empty: EAGAIN");
  close (u6);

  // SO_RCVTIMEO: a receive call that waits gives up after the time
  int u4 = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
  struct timeval tv = { 0, 300000 }, got;  socklen_t gl = sizeof got;
  CHECK (setsockopt (u4, SOL_SOCKET, SO_RCVTIMEO, &tv, sizeof tv) == 0 && getsockopt (u4, SOL_SOCKET, SO_RCVTIMEO, &got, &gl) == 0 && got.tv_sec == 0 && got.tv_usec == 300000,
         "SO_RCVTIMEO: set 300 ms, read back 300 ms");
  long t0 = now_ms ();
  errno = 0;
  n = recv (u4, buf, sizeof buf, 0);
  long took = now_ms () - t0;
  CHECK (n == -1 && errno == EAGAIN && took >= 250 && took < 3000, "recv with nothing to read: EAGAIN after about 300 ms (not at once, not for ever)");
  close (u4);

  // MSG_PEEK on a stream
  int s = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
  CHECK (connect (s, (struct sockaddr *) &tcp, sizeof tcp) == 0 && write (s, "hello\n", 6) == 6 && readable (s, 3000), "TCP: connected, \"hello\" sent, the answer has arrived");
  n = recv (s, buf, 3, MSG_PEEK);
  CHECK (n == 3 && !memcmp (buf, "HEL", 3), "recv (MSG_PEEK, 3 bytes) shows HEL");
  n = recv (s, buf, sizeof buf, MSG_PEEK);
  CHECK (n == 6 && !memcmp (buf, "HELLO\n", 6), "recv (MSG_PEEK) again shows all 6 bytes");
  CHECK (readable (s, 0), "poll: peeked bytes still count as readable");
  n = recv (s, buf, 4, 0);
  CHECK (n == 4 && !memcmp (buf, "HELL", 4), "recv takes 4 bytes from what was peeked");
  n = read (s, buf, sizeof buf);
  CHECK (n == 2 && !memcmp (buf, "O\n", 2), "read gives the 2 bytes that are left");
  shutdown (s, SHUT_WR);
  CHECK (read (s, buf, sizeof buf) == 0, "the end of the stream after the peek");
  close (s);

  // SO_ERROR: a fresh socket has none; a non-blocking connect that fails leaves ECONNREFUSED, read once
  int err = -1;  socklen_t el = sizeof err;
  s = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM | SOCK_NONBLOCK, 0);
  CHECK (getsockopt (s, SOL_SOCKET, SO_ERROR, &err, &el) == 0 && err == 0, "SO_ERROR of a new socket is 0");
  errno = 0;
  CHECK (connect (s, (struct sockaddr *) &closed, sizeof closed) == -1 && errno == EINPROGRESS, "non-blocking connect to a port where nobody listens: EINPROGRESS");
  struct pollfd pf = { s, POLLOUT, 0 };
  CHECK (poll (&pf, 1, 5000) == 1 && (pf.revents & POLLERR), "poll: the socket has POLLERR");
  err = -1;  el = sizeof err;
  CHECK (getsockopt (s, SOL_SOCKET, SO_ERROR, &err, &el) == 0 && err == ECONNREFUSED, "getsockopt (SO_ERROR): ECONNREFUSED");
  err = -1;  el = sizeof err;
  CHECK (getsockopt (s, SOL_SOCKET, SO_ERROR, &err, &el) == 0 && err == 0, "SO_ERROR again: 0 (reading it cleared it)");
  close (s);
  s = socket (AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM | SOCK_NONBLOCK, 0);
  connect (s, (struct sockaddr *) &tcp, sizeof tcp);
  pf.fd = s;
  err = -1;  el = sizeof err;
  CHECK (poll (&pf, 1, 5000) == 1 && (pf.revents & POLLOUT) && !(pf.revents & POLLERR) && getsockopt (s, SOL_SOCKET, SO_ERROR, &err, &el) == 0 && err == 0,
         "a non-blocking connect that works: POLLOUT, no POLLERR, SO_ERROR 0");
  close (s);
  return fails;
}
